  <header id="pf-top" class="pf-intro pf-reveal">
    <p class="pf-caption">
      A companion to the research article
      <a href="{{ pf.article_url }}"><em>The politics of baselines: A political feasibility framework for energy and climate scenarios</em></a> ·
      Fornborg (2026), <em>Energy Research &amp; Social Science</em>
    </p>
    <p class="pf-intro-lead">
      Energy and climate scenarios often rely on assumptions about future policy developments that are highly contested. Despite this, they are rarely
      screened for political feasibility before they enter models. This page demonstrates how a set of plausible policy responses to the rapid growth
      of datacenter electricity demand in Sweden can be identified, screened for political feasibility and carried into a power system model.
    </p>
  </header>

  <figure class="pf-method pf-fw pf-reveal" aria-labelledby="pf-framework-title">
    <figcaption>
      <h2 id="pf-framework-title">The framework</h2>
      <p>
        Following Kahneman and Lovallo (1993) and Jewell and Cherp (2023), the framework combines an outside view with an inside view. The outside
        view allows a scoping of plausible policy trajectories through spatial and temporal comparison, while the inside view enables an assessment of
        the feasibility of trajectories informed by political economy and institutional theory.
      </p>
    </figcaption>
    <div class="pf-fw-grid">
