  <section id="pf-trajectories" class="pf-section pf-reveal">
    <p class="pf-step-tag">From the outside view</p>
    <h2><span class="pf-section-number">03</span> Define comparable trajectories</h2>
    <p class="pf-prose">{{ pf.derivation }}</p>
    {% include policy-feasibility/trajectory-cards.liquid %}
    <div class="pf-drawers">
      <details class="pf-drawer" id="pf-on-additionality">
        <summary><strong>On additionality</strong><span>What T1b adds to T1, what research says and how it is used here</span></summary>
        <div class="pf-drawer-body pf-additionality-note">
          <p>
            Under T1b, the new generation must also be additional. Generation is additional if it would not have been built without the buyer’s demand
            or contract. A power purchase agreement (PPA) for fossil-free electricity shows that a buyer has contracted fossil-free generation. It
            does not by itself show that the generation is new to the system. If the plant would have been built anyway, the contract changes who
            claims its output, not how much fossil-free electricity is produced.
          </p>
          <p>
            Research on corporate procurement points the same way. Bjørn et al. (2022) find that companies using renewable energy certificates
            reported emission cuts about three times larger than their actual cuts, because buying certificates rarely changes how much renewable
            electricity is produced. Model studies of the US power system find that matching annual use with clean generation has little effect on
            emissions in the system as a whole (Xu et al., 2024). Matching use hour by hour with new generation reduces emissions more, at a higher
            cost (Xu et al., 2024; Riepin and Brown, 2024).
          </p>
          <p>
            The idea already has a place in EU law. Renewable hydrogen must be produced with electricity from installations that started operating at
            most 36 months before the electrolyser, matched monthly and, from 2030, hour by hour (Delegated Regulation (EU) 2023/1184). For
            datacenters, Fingrid has argued that moving existing resources between contracts does not add capacity, and EcoDataCenter favours
            requirements that lead to new generation. Svenska kraftnät’s proposal is broader and concerns fossil-free generation in general.
          </p>
          <p>
            In this demonstration, the limit on onshore wind decides much of the answer. The model limits onshore wind in Sweden to a level that
            corresponds to 70 TWh a year, the lower of the Swedish Energy Agency’s estimates. Because the model builds up to this limit in all runs,
            new onshore wind under T1 is mostly wind that would have been built anyway. With a higher limit, the result could differ. T1b therefore
            counts only generation that the model would not otherwise build in Sweden, which is offshore wind and new nuclear power.
          </p>
          <p>
            Additionality is hard to verify, since it requires a judgement about what would have happened otherwise. In practice, rules use proxies
            such as the age of the plant, the absence of subsidies or a link to a specific new project. The definition used here is a modelling
            choice, not a settled standard.
          </p>
          <ul class="pf-lit">
            <li>
              Bjørn, A., Lloyd, S. M., Brander, M. and Matthews, H. D. (2022). Renewable energy certificates threaten the integrity of corporate
              science-based targets. <em>Nature Climate Change</em>, 12, 539–546.
              <a href="https://doi.org/10.1038/s41558-022-01379-5">doi:10.1038/s41558-022-01379-5</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              Xu, Q., Ricks, W., Manocha, A., Patankar, N. and Jenkins, J. D. (2024). System-level impacts of voluntary carbon-free electricity
              procurement strategies. <em>Joule</em>, 8(2), 374–400.
              <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.joule.2023.12.007">doi:10.1016/j.joule.2023.12.007</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              Riepin, I. and Brown, T. (2024). On the means, costs, and system-level impacts of 24/7 carbon-free energy procurement.
              <em>Energy Strategy Reviews</em>. <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.07876">arXiv:2403.07876</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              Commission Delegated Regulation (EU) 2023/1184 on rules for renewable fuels of non-biological origin.
              <a href="https://eur-lex.europa.eu/eli/reg_del/2023/1184/oj">EUR-Lex</a>
            </li>
          </ul>
          {% assign add_ids = 'E28,E13,E08' | split: ',' %}
          {% include policy-feasibility/references.liquid ids=add_ids %}
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>
